The ink buffer gets populated with the two bytes of the 16-bit value, but then this buffer is reinterpreted as a 32-bit signed integer when converted to Python. On a little-endian system, this would actually reconstruct the original value correctly since the low byte is at the lowest memory address. But the problem is that the ink is being treated as a signed integer when it should be treated as an unsigned 16-bit value.

Looking at the debug output showing `0x34` instead of `0x1234`, it appears the high byte is being lost somewhere in this conversion chain. Let me check the drawing functions to see how they're extracting the color value.

